{"reference":"1 Samuel 2","book":"1 Samuel","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Hannah’s Song","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Hannah’s prayer praises the LORD as the one who overturns ordinary human rankings: the proud are judged, the mighty lose strength, the hungry are filled, and the poor are raised up. Its closing lines also speak of the LORD giving strength to his king and exalting his anointed.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 2:1-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_002_ae956054b202c74ddd3d0e1c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Movement of 1 Samuel","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The wider book moves from the failure of Eli’s priestly house through Samuel’s leadership, Saul’s rejected kingship, and David’s anointing, rise, exile, and Saul’s death. This gives the chapter’s contrast between Eli’s household and the growing Samuel a place within the book’s larger movement.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_002_5cc5e36898f4189b67bbd4b"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}